WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.840
Dorothy Howell: The Agnes Morris House had an ironer, a rolling ironer.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.300
You don’t know what that is, do you?

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:08.500
Jennifer Abraham: No, I don’t.

4
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.820
Howell: Well, you put your sheets through it; all the sheets were ironed.

5
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.120
You know, there were no cottons that didn’t wrinkle.

6
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:23.880
Everything had to be ironed, and so you’d run all the household linens through the ironer and you had to learn to use it.

7
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.020
Had to learn to use the washing machine.

8
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.260
You had to know how to take out stains.

9
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.940
Meal management, you had to know how to entertain guests.

10
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:43.160
You would invite people to come to dinner once a week and you would be expected to know how to meet them at the door,

11
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.380
how to seat them at the table, how to set the table properly, how to be a good hostess.

12
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:58.000
And the home management houses were really good at sort of preparing you for life.

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:07.020
We had to . . . We didn’t have money for flowers but we’d go all over the campus and pick different kinds of greenery to decorate the place.

14
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.600
They’re skills that I really have enjoyed all my life.
